<description>
Move each maroon marker toward the blue rectangle and recolor the rectangle red.

Input structure
A background canvas contains one solid blue rectangular object and several isolated
maroon marker pixels. Each marker is horizontally or vertically aligned with the
rectangle and lies outside it.

Rule
Start with a background canvas, repaint the blue rectangle red, and move each
maroon marker straight toward the rectangle along its row or column. Exactly one
marker is strictly closer to the rectangle than all others and enters the
rectangle boundary; every other marker lands in the adjacent exterior cell just
outside its aligned side.

Key insight
The single entering marker is the unique global nearest marker to the rectangle,
not the nearest marker on each of the four sides.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "move_along_path",
  "src": "isolated maroon marker pixels aligned by row or column with the solid blue rectangle",
  "path": "straight cardinal path from each marker toward its aligned rectangle side; stop on the side cell if the marker is closer than that side's span, otherwise stop in the adjacent exterior cell",
  "trajectory": "move only the marker pixel to the path endpoint",
  "dst": "blue rectangle side cell or adjacent exterior halo cell selected by the stopping rule",
  "transform": "erase the original markers and recolor the blue rectangle red"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 8-16 rows x 8-16 cols, always square [observed]
  background: ORANGE (7)
  rectangle:
    color: BLUE (1) in input, becomes RED (2) in output
    shape: solid square, side length 3-5
    grid-to-rect ratio: 2.5-3.2 [observed]
    position: anywhere; may touch grid edges
  markers:
    color: MAROON (9)
    count: 2-7 isolated single pixels
    alignment: each marker shares a row or column with the rectangle interior (aligned to exactly one side)
    no two markers share same alignment lane (same col for above/below, same row for left/right)
    sides used: 2-4 distinct sides; a side is used only if its margin >= 1
    distance: 2-7 cells from nearest rect edge along alignment axis
    global_entry_limit: REQUIRED; exactly one marker total is close enough to land inside the rectangle, and it is the unique globally nearest marker among all markers
  stopping_rule:
    entering marker: the unique globally nearest marker has dist < rect_side and lands ON the rect boundary cell (inside rect area)
    non-entering markers: every other marker has dist >= rect_side and lands on the exterior halo cell (one cell outside rect)
    nearest_only: choose one unique nearest marker globally; do not choose a nearest marker per side
  mix: each example has exactly 1 close/entering marker and at least 1 far/exterior marker [observed]
  output: erase original markers, recolor rect RED, place each marker at its destination; all destinations unique

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size N in [8..16] (square). Pick rect side S in [3..5] with N/S in [2..4].
    2. Place the S x S rectangle at random position ensuring at least 1 cell margin on >= 2 sides.
    3. For each side with margin >= 1, decide how many markers (0 to side_span) ensuring total in [2..7].
    4. For each marker, pick a unique lane (col for above/below, row for left/right) within rect span.
    5. Choose exactly one global entering marker (on boundary); all other markers use exterior halo destinations.
    6. Compute valid source position by inverting the stopping rule: entering -> pick a unique smallest dist in [2..S-1]; far -> pick dist in [S..margin]. Reject any tie for the global minimum.
    7. Verify all destinations are unique and all source positions are in-bounds and distinct.
    8. Build the input grid: fill background, paint rect BLUE, place markers at source positions.
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

